    "findings": [
      "No demonstrated Vulkan/CUDA ownership defect; concurrent vkDestroyDevice and NVIDIA thread-local cleanup are the strongest localization, but the proprietary lock cycle is unproved.",
      "A C++ destructor mutex does not cover later vendor TLS cleanup; retirement through OS join covers owned worker threads.",
      "eglReleaseThread is not a proven Vulkan TLS barrier and may change embedding EGL state.",
      "An unmodified GLX control is legitimate, although the external review favored the join diagnostic first."
    ],
    "implementation_decision": "A suggested startup mutex was not adopted because partially constructed worker resources can unwind before thread TLS exits. The implementation instead starts workers sequentially through readiness and joins a failed newest thread before earlier workers retire.",
    "subsequent_evidence": "The later local GLX reproduction disproves ICD selection alone as a sufficient mitigation for the exercised fixture; it does not prove which proprietary locks form the stall."
  },
